---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const app = 'https://materials-ml-predictor.streamlit.app/';
---
<Base title="Materials property predictor" description="A machine learning model that predicts whether a compound is a metal and estimates its band gap from the formula alone, with a live app.">
  <article class="case">
    <a class="case-back" href="/projects">All projects</a>
    <p class="case-kind">Personal project, started October 2025</p>
    <h1>Materials property predictor</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">A model that takes a chemical formula and predicts whether the compound is a metal and what its band gap is, trained on nearly 5,000 real, experimentally measured compounds and available as a live app.</p>

    <figure class="case-viewer">
      <div class="viewer-box app-box" id="appbox">
        <div class="app-poster">
          <p class="app-name">Materials property predictor</p>
          <p class="app-sub">Type a formula such as GaN or Fe2O3 and see the prediction.</p>
          <button class="btn solid" id="load-app">Open the app here</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The app runs on Streamlit and can take a few seconds to wake up the first time. <a href={app} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it in a new tab</a></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Everything, from the data and features to the models and the app</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>Python, scikit-learn, Streamlit</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>4,921 compounds for metal or non-metal, 4,604 with measured band gaps</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Results</dt><dd>91.9% accuracy on metal or non-metal, band gap error of 0.40 eV</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>How it started</h2>
    <p>This began as a university computing challenge on a synthetic dataset, where my models scored 100%. When I looked into why, I found that a single column, the band gap, decided every label exactly while the other seven measurements were random noise, which meant the perfect score said very little about whether the approach would work on real materials.</p>

    <h2>Rebuilding it on real data</h2>
    <p>I rebuilt the project on experimental data, using metal and non-metal labels for 4,921 compounds and measured band gaps for 4,604 of them, taken from Zhuo, Mansouri Tehrani and Brgoch (2018) as curated in the Matbench benchmark. The model only needs the chemical formula, so I turned each formula into 112 numbers describing its elements, such as the weighted average, range and spread of 22 properties like electronegativity and atomic radius, following the approach of Ward et al. (2016).</p>

    <h2>Choosing a model</h2>
    <p>I compared gradient-boosted decision trees against logistic regression, a random forest and a simple baseline, and the gradient-boosted trees performed best. Every score comes from 5-fold cross-validation, so each compound is predicted by a model that never saw it during training.</p>

    <h2>How well it works</h2>
    <p>The model tells metals from non-metals with 91.9% accuracy, where guessing would score 50%. For band gaps its average error is 0.40 eV compared with 1.14 eV for always guessing the average, and it reaches an R² of 0.72.</p>

    <h2>Where it falls short</h2>
    <p>Because it only sees the formula, it cannot tell apart different structures of the same compound, and it underestimates very wide band gaps, predicting about 4 eV for aluminium oxide when the measured value is closer to 8.8 eV, since few training compounds have gaps that large. It is also unreliable for pure elements, which are missing from the training data, and for unusual chemistries far from anything it has seen.</p>
  </article>
  <script>
    document.getElementById('load-app')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const box = document.getElementById('appbox')!;
      const f = document.createElement('iframe');
      f.src = 'https://materials-ml-predictor.streamlit.app/?embed=true'; f.title = 'Materials property predictor app';
      box.replaceChildren(f);
    });
  </script>
</Base>
